  completeExplanation: 'The chapter establishes the fundamental geographical principle that human life is inextricably linked to the physical environment. In the fertile plains of Punjab, flat terrain and rich soils foster intensive agriculture and community traditions like Langar Seva at the Golden Temple. In the arid Thar desert of Rajasthan, extreme water scarcity drives the evolution of specialized water storage (tankas), thick-walled mud architecture, and reliance on camels ("the ship of the desert"). In the coastal region of Puri, Odisha, proximity to the sea creates fishing livelihoods, rich marine biodiversity, temple arts (shankh blowing, Rath Yatra), and the necessity for cyclone disaster management. In the rugged Himalayan mountains of Sikkim, steep slopes and cold temperatures necessitate warm woollen attire (bakhu, dumdyam), sturdy wooden homes with steep sloping roofs to shed snow and rain, yak transport, and vibrant New Year harvest celebrations (Loosong with Chaam mask dances). Through these four case studies, students learn that human diversity in food, clothing, housing, and culture represents creative adaptation to varying landscapes.',
  formulasRules: [
    'Core Adaptation Rule: Landform + Climate + Resources -> Human Adaptation -> Diet, Clothing, Housing, Occupations & Culture',
    'Plains Rule: Flat Land + Fertile Soil -> Farming -> Wheat, Mustard, Maize & Dairy Diet',
    'Desert Rule: Arid Climate + Scanty Rain -> Water Conservation (Tankas) + Camels + Mud Houses with Thatched Roofs',
    'Coast Rule: Sea Margin -> Fishing Livelihoods + Marine Biodiversity + Cyclone Disaster Management',
    'Mountain Rule: High Altitude + Cold & Steep Slopes -> Sloping Roofs (Snow/Rain Runoff) + Yaks + Warm Attire (Bakhu)',
  ],
  storiesExamples: [
